  "text": "Can you tell the difference between these two cards? Well, you can't. But this guy can. And he's about to win millions just because of this tiny printing mistake. It's undetectable to the average eye, but Phil Ivey accidentally noticed that one brand of cards has this fatal flaw. During the production process of cutting these cards, the cuts were not perfectly aligned, making the ends of the pattern slightly different on both edges. But how do you even get the dealer to use that specific brand of cards? Well, Phil told the dealer that he wanted that one deck because it brought him good luck. And since Ivy was a high roller, the dealer agreed. But how do you even use it to your advantage? Well, after each play, the gambler asked the dealer to rotate the high value cards 180 degrees. And when he asked why, Ivy played dumb again and said it's because of his superstition. The dealer was confused, but had no idea how this could benefit the player, so he complied. Repeat that many times till you've gone through the entire deck, and now all the cards are sorted. hence the name edge sorting. Now Ivy could distinguish the best cards before they were revealed. This gave him a 5% advantage in Baccarat and that was just enough for him to win nearly 22 million dollars over a few months. However after the casinos checked the tapes and realized what he had done two casinos sued him and he was forced to give back all that money for quote failing to provide the casino with a fair gaming experience. So is this actually cheating? Or was he just punished for having really good vision?"
